Bring up the Secure Enclave (SEP) on Apple t6020 (MacBook Pro 14" M2 Pro).

Enables the SEP IOMMU (sep_dart) and the sep node, and adds a "sep" alias.
The alias lets m1n1 dt_set_sep() inject the SEPFW reserved-memory region and
the iboot/local-policy manifests; the DART is required for dma_map_resource()
of that region. Missing alias -> -EINVAL (-22); missing DART -> -ENOMEM (-12).
Touch ID reverse-engineering, step 3 (~/Projects/MacFingerprint).

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t6020-j414s.dts	2026-06-11 08:30:31.670670565 +0200
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t6020-j414s.dts	2026-06-11 08:30:31.678837032 +0200
@@ -45,3 +45,23 @@
 &mtp_mt {
 	firmware-name = "apple/tpmtfw-j414s.bin";
 };
+
+/* Touch ID RE (step 3): bring up the SEP boot stub.
+ * - sep_dart (its IOMMU) and the sep node itself ship disabled -> enable both.
+ * - add the "sep" alias so m1n1 dt_set_sep() injects the sepfw reserved-memory
+ *   region + iboot/local-policy manifests.
+ * Without the DART, dma_map_resource() of sepfw fails -> -ENOMEM (-12);
+ * without the alias, probe fails earlier with -EINVAL (-22). */
+&sep_dart {
+	status = "okay";
+};
+
+&sep {
+	status = "okay";
+};
+
+/ {
+	aliases {
+		sep = &sep;
+	};
+};
